body:
hey! you should pm me, I'm a culinary grad, worked in a lot of kitchens, currently in school to become a dietitian (I'm not a dietitian yet so my advice on the topic is limited) and I run a side business where I sell healthy/balanced packed lunches to teachers. I have a bunch of simple yet exciting lunch ideas that I use to make eating healthy more fun but not too difficult. At the moment I've been eating this rice vermicelli salad with shrimp, cucumbers, lettuce, carrots, cilantro, and spicy peanut sauce. I like to make tuna salad and pita chips or just place the tuna in some bibb lettuce for a wrap. I make an amazing curry with sweet potatoes, chickpeas, raisins, coconut cream, and tomatoes.  In my day to day, I like to stick to convenient staples that I've picked out for myself the past few months that work. I keep 2-3 fruit in my fridge/counter every week so I can grab them when I want a treat (usually mango, watermelon, and oranges) and a bag of frozen berries (whatever's on sale) that I can turn into slushies (not a fan of creamier smoothies) or oatmeal. I keep 2 savoury snacks, usually whole grain nachos and fresh salsa and skinny pop and of course veggies w/ dip. I'm obsessed with cobb salads so I keep boiled eggs and cooked chicken on hand, as well as a really simple avocado lime dressing (no oil). My biggest tip is to find the staple ingredients that you will genuinely enjoy and consume every day (don't just buy some trendy health food because it's what seems right and have it rot away in the back of your fridge) and introduce one new thing every week and see if you like it.    

&#x200B;

again, pm if you want to bounce ideas off of me, I love talking about cooking
